<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trinity College Dublin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1592">1592</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Trinity College <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> is the college founded in 1592 by a charter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> on the site of a dissolved monastery in Dublin. For centuries the intellectual centre of the Protestant Ascendancy, it educated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonathan-swift">Jonathan Swift</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-berkeley">George Berkeley</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-goldsmith">Oliver Goldsmith</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>, and its Old Library holds the ninth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-kells">Book of Kells</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trinity-college-dublin.jpg" alt="Trinity College Dublin" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stephen Bergin steve_ · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_entrance_of_the_historic_Trinity_College_(Unsplash).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-dublin">University of Dublin</a> was founded in 1320 under a papal brief issued by Pope Clement V in 1311. It kept an intermittent existence at St Patrick's Cathedral but did not flourish and ended during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. In 1592 a small group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> citizens obtained letters patent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> incorporating Trinity College on the site of the disbanded Augustinian Priory of All Hallows, southeast of the city walls, which the Corporation of Dublin provided.</p><p>The college was established to consolidate Tudor rule in Ireland. Provost Adam Loftus named it after Trinity College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and it was founded on the model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and Cambridge. It was the only college ever established within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-dublin">University of Dublin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 18th century Trinity was the university of the Protestant Ascendancy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Parliament of Ireland</a>, meeting across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-green">College Green</a>, made generous grants for the neoclassical Parliament Square, whose first building was the Old Library, begun in 1712, followed by the Printing House and the Dining Hall. The library has housed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-kells">Book of Kells</a> since 1661. Trinity gained the world's oldest chairs in French and German (1776), Irish (1840) and English literature (1867).</p><p>Women were admitted as full members for the first time in 1904. From 1904 to 1907 women from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, who were not granted degrees there, came to Trinity to receive them; known as the Steamboat ladies, they paid fees that helped to fund Trinity Hall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The college is governed by the Provost, Fellows and Scholars under letters patent that later monarchs amended. Charles I in 1637 raised the number of fellows from seven to 16 and established the Board. The university was linked to parliamentary representation from 1613, when James I granted it two seats in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-house-of-commons">Irish House of Commons</a>; the vote was first limited to the Provost, Fellows and Scholars, extended in 1832 to holders of an MA and in 1918 to all graduates. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1800">Acts of Union 1800</a> it elected members to Westminster, among them W. E. H. Lecky and Edward Carson.</p><p>Trinity is a sister college of Oriel College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, and St John's College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and a graduate of any of the three universities may be incorporated into another. Traditions include Commons, a meal in the Dining Hall begun by the slamming of its doors, and Trinity Week in April. The student society The Hist was founded in 1770, and from 1869 to 1893 the college's literary magazine Kottabos was edited by Robert Yelverton Tyrrell.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trinity College Dublin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Protestant Ireland produced an intellectual culture of its own: <b>Trinity College Dublin</b> was founded in 1592, and Jonathan Swift, George Berkeley, Oliver Goldsmith and Edmund Burke were among its writers and thinkers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Stolen from Kells in 1007 and recovered, it went to <b>Trinity College Dublin</b> in 1661 and was added to UNESCO's Memory of the World Register in 2011.</blockquote><a href="/work/book-of-kells">Book of Kells · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
